<p>I’ll place my hand on a Bible and vow to tell the whole truth and nothing but. I’m not a smart guy. I admit it freely. On a good day, I can probably squeeze into the fat part of the bell curve.</p>
<p>And yet, in social circumstances, people think I’m some kind of genius simply because I read books.</p>
<p>It’s true. <b>If you read, people automatically think you’re smarter than you actually are.</b></p>
<p>Reading, something quite common as little as 50 years ago, has become one of life’s hidden cheat codes. If you can read as little as 3 to 5 books a year you certainly won’t be a genius but you could easily play one on TV. And quite convincingly too.</p>
<p>People suddenly listen more intently when you speak, and you don’t get interrupted as frequently. If you do get interrupted, you can confidently say, “Excuse me, just let me finish this thought.”</p>
<p>The amount of public consumption of books has dropped <i>that</i> low.</p>
<p>And yet, with the availability of reading materials today it’s never been easier to develop a voracious reading habit. That’s what this article is about.</p>
<p>We’ll look at:</p>
